WEBVTT

1
00:00.400 --> 00:05.210
This is the example web app for the Db2 for i MCP Server.

2
00:05.280 --> 00:09.590
Your team asks questions about IBM i data in a browser,

3
00:09.640 --> 00:12.410
without an AI subscription for each person.

4
00:13.260 --> 00:17.610
Everyone signs in with their own IBM i user profile.

5
00:17.660 --> 00:23.430
Every query runs with that user's authority, and the server's checks still apply.

6
00:24.180 --> 00:26.317
Ask in plain language.

7
00:26.367 --> 00:30.770
The model writes a read-only query, the server checks it and runs it,

8
00:30.820 --> 00:34.110
and the answer comes back with the rows behind it.

9
00:35.260 --> 00:37.970
Every result opens as a table.

10
00:38.020 --> 00:40.970
Sort it, filter it, copy it for Excel,

11
00:41.020 --> 00:44.070
or export every row to a file.

12
00:46.220 --> 00:48.810
Ask for a chart, and you get one.

13
00:48.860 --> 00:51.370
The chart view comes from the server itself,

14
00:51.420 --> 00:56.390
as an MCP App, so it draws the actual rows from the query.

15
00:59.340 --> 01:01.690
Bars stacked by status.

16
01:01.740 --> 01:04.310
A pareto chart for your top customers.

17
01:04.540 --> 01:07.270
A waterfall from last year to this year.

18
01:07.500 --> 01:09.190
A doughnut for shares.

19
01:09.580 --> 01:12.710
Switch the chart type, or go back to the table.

20
01:15.660 --> 01:19.317
Business tools your IT team wrote show up as forms,

21
01:19.367 --> 01:21.957
so you can run one without the model.

22
01:22.007 --> 01:26.470
The context page shows the table notes the model reads first.

23
01:29.620 --> 01:34.850
The same chart view works in Claude and other apps that support MCP Apps.

24
01:34.900 --> 01:36.690
And you choose the model:

25
01:36.740 --> 01:42.990
a pay-as-you-go key, or a local model that keeps questions and results inside your network.

26
01:45.240 --> 01:48.470
The web app is an example in the repository.

27
01:48.520 --> 01:51.730
Read it, run it, and make it your own.
